#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Mock-backed behaviour evals, written from scenarios with a clock each scenario sets.

The text-only cases in plugins/ai-workforce/evals/ give their inputs as prose and have no apps
connected, so none of them sees a tool being used. The cases written here stand in for the
owner's apps with the eval tool's mocks (`claude plugin eval`, "Mock MCP servers"), so a grader
can check what the team actually called and saved, not only what it said.

    python3 tools/eval_scenarios.py          # (re)write every generated case
    python3 tools/eval_scenarios.py --check  # fail if a generated file is missing, stale or stray

How the stand-ins work
- Every case carries its own `mocks/` (Notion is the plugin's own server; Gmail, Google_Calendar and
  scheduled-tasks are stand-alone ones). There is deliberately no suite-wide `evals/mocks/`: it
  would add tools to the text-only cases too, and those were validated without any.
- Mocks are fixed answers, so a stand-in cannot change between two calls in one run. Reads return
  the scenario's whole state (the Business Brain on one page; every thread and draft in one
  answer). Writes return a one-line marker (SENT-RECEIPT, DRAFT-SAVED, NOTION-UPDATED ...) that
  graders count with `target: mock_calls`.
- The clock is the "Evaluation clock" line in each case's system prompt. A scenario that spans
  several runs is several cases over the same state with the clock moved (see RUNS below).

Nothing here is from a real business: the owner, customers and addresses are invented.
Run the cases with `claude plugin eval` (they cost usage; see tests/README.md).
"""
